explanation:
For alignment purposes, an inline `<svg>` and an `<img>` behave the same: both are `display: inline` atomic boxes whose bottom edge sits on the baseline, so both leave the familiar gap underneath and both respond to `vertical-align`.

The difference is styling. Inline SVG is part of the page's DOM, so page CSS reaches its shapes: `fill: currentColor` inherits the text color, and hover/focus color changes work. An SVG referenced by `<img src>` is an isolated document; page CSS can't style its insides, so `currentColor` there resolves to black.

A common icon recipe:

```css
.icon {
  width: 1em;
  height: 1em;
  fill: currentColor;
  vertical-align: -0.125em;
}
```

