snippets.code:
body {
  line-height: 1.6;
}

.article {
  line-height: 1.8;
  font: 1.125rem Georgia, serif;
}

explanation:
`font` is a shorthand for `font-style`, `font-variant`, `font-weight`, `font-stretch`, `font-size`, `line-height` and `font-family`. Any part you leave out is reset to its initial value, and the initial value of `line-height` is `normal`.

Both declarations are in the same rule with the same specificity, so the later one wins: `.article` ends up with `line-height: normal`, and the paragraphs inherit `normal`. The `1.6` on `body` doesn't matter either, because `.article` sets its own value.

`normal` is chosen by the browser from the font's metrics, so it varies by font and platform. For common text fonts it's roughly 1.1 to 1.35 (Georgia, for example, comes out at about 1.14 in Chrome on Windows). That's tighter than 1.6 or 1.8, and it's why a unitless number is the more predictable choice.

Fixes:

```css
.article { font: 1.125rem/1.8 Georgia, serif; }
/* or put line-height after the shorthand */
.article { font: 1.125rem Georgia, serif; line-height: 1.8; }
```

