snippets.code:
.alert {
  background-color: rgb(255, 240, 200);
  padding: 12px;
}

.alert.warning {
  background: url("warning.svg") no-repeat left center;
  padding-left: 32px;
}

explanation:
A shorthand always sets **all** of its longhands. Any longhand you leave out is set to its initial value. So `background: url("warning.svg") no-repeat left center` also declares `background-color: transparent` (plus `background-size: auto`, `background-attachment: scroll`, and so on).

That hidden `background-color: transparent` comes from the more specific `.alert.warning` rule, so it beats `.alert`'s cream color. The same thing happens inside a single rule when the shorthand comes after the longhand.

Fixes:
- Use longhands for what you actually want to change:
```css
.alert.warning {
  background-image: url("warning.svg");
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
  background-position: left center;
}
```
- Or repeat the color in the shorthand: `background: rgb(255, 240, 200) url("warning.svg") no-repeat left center;`

The same trap exists with `font` (resets `line-height`), `margin`/`padding`, `border`, and `flex`.

