snippets.code:
.hero { height: 300px; }
nav {
  position: sticky;
  height: 50px;
}
main { height: 3000px; }

explanation:
`position: sticky` needs at least one inset that isn't `auto` (`top`, `bottom`, `left`, `right`, or the logical `inset-*` equivalents). That inset is the threshold: "keep this edge at least this far from the scrollport's edge." With every inset `auto`, the element never sticks and behaves just like `position: relative`.

Measured in Chrome after `scrollTo(0, 500)`: without `top`, the nav's top is at -192px (8px default body margin + 300px hero - 500px, scrolled away). With `top: 0` added, it's at 0px.

Fix:

```css
nav {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
}
```

