snippets.code:
.layout {
  display: flex;
  gap: 24px;
}
.sidebar {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
  width: 200px;
}
.main {
  flex: 1;
  height: 3000px; /* stands in for long content */
}

explanation:
The default `align-items: stretch` makes the sidebar as tall as the tallest item in the row: 3000px in Chrome, the same as `.main`. A sticky element can't leave its containing block (`.layout`), and the sidebar already fills it completely, so there's no room to slide. It scrolls away (top = -492px after `scrollTo(0, 500)`, i.e. the default 8px body margin minus 500px).

Fix: stop the stretch so the sidebar is only as tall as its content.

```css
.sidebar {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
  align-self: flex-start; /* or align-items: flex-start on .layout */
}
```

With that, the sidebar is only 18px tall in the test page and sits at 0px after scrolling 500px. The same trap happens in grid layouts, where items stretch by default too.

